{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /local/home/scheschb/.codex/plugins/cache/aim/AmazonBuilderCoreAIAgents-core/1.0.6505240713/skills/brazil/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /local/home/scheschb/.codex/plugins/cache/aim/AmazonBuilderCoreAIAgents-core/1.0.6505240713/skills/brazil/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: brazil\ndescription: Amazon's internal build system for Java/Kotlin (brazil-gradle), TypeScript/JS (npm-pretty-much), Python (brazil-python), Rust (CargoBrazil), Go, C/C++ (brazil-cmake). Use when working with Brazil packages, workspaces, dependencies, version sets, build commands, or encountering build errors and dependency conflicts.\nversion: 1.0.0\ntags: [skill, brazil, build-system, dependencies, java, kotlin, typescript, javascript, python, go, rust, c-cpp, cargobrazil, cargo-brazil]\n---\n\n# Brazil Build System\n\n## Overview\nBrazil is Amazon's internal build system providing a unified interface for diverse build technologies (npm-pretty-much, brazil-gradle, brazil-python, etc.). It manages dependencies, coordinates package versions through version sets, and supports reproducible builds across languages.\n\n**Main Documentation:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/user-guide/\n\n## Usage\nWhen working with Amazon internal development and encountering:\n- Package creation, configuration, or dependency management\n- Workspace setup or build errors\n- Version conflicts or dependency resolution issues\n- Language-specific build system configuration\n- Publishing packages or setting up CI/CD\n- Commands like `brazil-build`, `brazil workspace`, `brazil versionset`\n\n## Quick Start\n\n### 1. Identify the Language First\nCheck the package's `Config` file for `build-system` to determine which language guide to reference:\n\n- `brazil-gradle`, `happytrails`, `happiertrails` → **[Java/Kotlin](references/language/java-kotlin.md)**\n- `npm-pretty-much` → **[TypeScript/JavaScript](references/language/typescript-javascript.md)**\n- `brazil-python` → **[Python](references/language/python.md)**\n- `brazil-cmake` → **[C/C++](references/language/c-cpp.md)**\n- `bgo-wrap-make` → **[Go](references/language/go.md)**\n- `cargo-brazil-build` → **[Rust](references/language/rust.md)**\n\nYou **MUST** reference the appropriate language guide for language-specific commands, dependencies, and troubleshooting.\n\n### 2. Reference Workflow Guides as Needed\n- **[Workspace Setup](references/workspace-setup.md)** - Creating workspaces, using packages, workspace structure\n- **[Package Management](references/package-management.md)** - Creating packages, configuring dependencies, publishing\n- **[Dependency Management](references/dependency-management.md)** - Resolving conflicts, debugging dependencies, Gordian Knot\n- **[Build and Test](references/build-and-test.md)** - Building packages, running tests, build output\n- **[Version Sets](references/version-sets.md)** - Creating or managing version sets\n- **[CLI Commands](references/cli-commands.md)** - Comprehensive Brazil CLI command reference\n\n## Core Concepts\n\n### Packages\nThe basic unit of code in Brazil containing source code, build configuration, and dependency information.\n\n**Key characteristics:**\n- Each package is a separate git repository\n- Lives in workspace `src/` directory\n- Owned by teams via bindles, not individuals\n\n**For details on package structure and Config files:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/user-guide/concepts-package/ and https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/user-guide/concepts-config-file/\n\n### Workspaces\nContainer for one or more packages you're working on, providing isolation and controlled build environment.\n\n**Structure:**\n```\nWorkspaceName/\n├── src/\n│   ├── PackageA/  (git repo)\n│   └── PackageB/  (git repo)\n└── [workspace metadata]\n```\n\n**Critical rule:** For single-package builds, you **MUST** navigate into the specific package directory (`cd src/PackageName`) before running build commands. For building all packages, use `brazil-recursive-cmd --allPackages brazil-build` from any package in the Brazil workspace instead.\n\n### Version Sets\nCollections of package versions that are known to work together, enabling consistent builds across environments.\n\n**Purpose:**\n- Define package versions that work together\n- Coordinate dependency updates across teams\n- Enable consistent builds\n\n**For details:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/user-guide/concepts-version-set/\n\n### Dependencies\nBrazil manages package dependencies to ensure required code is available during build and runtime.\n\n**Dependency Models:**\n- **Classic Brazil**: Dependencies declared in Config file - https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/user-guide/\n- **Peru**: Language-native dependency management (e.g., package.json for npm, requirements.txt for Python) - https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/peru-user-guide/\n\n**Types:**\n- **Regular dependencies**: Required for compilation/runtime, exported to consumers\n- **Test dependencies**: Only for testing, not exported\n- **Runtime dependencies**: Required at runtime but not compile time\n- **Conflict resolution**: Use `resolves-conflict-dependencies` to specify versions when conflicts occur\n\n**For details:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/user-guide/concepts-dependencies/\n\n### Build Systems\nBrazil supports multiple language-specific build systems. The most common are:\n- **Java/Kotlin**: brazil-gradle, happytrails/happiertrails\n- **TypeScript/JavaScript**: npm-pretty-much\n- **Python**: brazil-python\n- **Ruby**: brazil-rake\n- **Go**: bgo-wrap-make\n- **Rust**: brazil-cargo\n- **C/C++**: brazil-cmake\n- **Scala**: brazil-sbt, happiertrails\n\nSome packages use wrapper build systems (e.g., `cdk-build`) or fully custom ones (e.g., `custom-build`). If the build system is not recognized, check the package README or ask the user for guidance on how to build.\n\n## Common Workflows\n\n### Building an Existing Package in a workspace\n**When user already has a workspace with the package:**\n1. Confirm you are in a brazil workspace with `brazil workspace show`\n2. Navigate to package: `cd src/PackageName`\n3. Build: `brazil-build release`\n\n**To build all packages at once with the release target** (note: the same can be done with other targets like `test`):\n```bash\nbrazil-recursive-cmd --allPackages brazil-build release\n```\n\n### Creating a New Workspace from Scratch and building\n**Only when creating a new workspace:**\n1. Find a package's primary version set at https://code.amazon.com/packages/PackageName/releases using ReadInternalWebsite\n2. Create workspace: `brazil workspace create --name MyWorkspace --versionSet MyVersionSetGroup/MyVersionSetName`. You **MUST** specify a version set, ask the user if you cannot find a primary version set.\n3. Use package: `brazil workspace use --package PackageName`\n4. Navigate to package: `cd src/PackageName`\n5. Build: `brazil-build release`\n\n### Resolving Dependency Conflicts\n1. Identify conflict via build error\n2. Use Gordian Knot: `gk-fix-mv-conflicts`\n3. Or manually add to Config:\n   ```\n   resolves-conflict-dependencies = {\n       1.0 = {\n           ConflictPackage = 2.x;\n       };\n   };\n   ```\n4. Rebuild: `brazil-build release`\n\n### Adding Dependencies\n1. Find package: https://code.amazon.com/packages/search\n2. Find package major version number at https://code.amazon.com/packages/PackageName/releases using ReadInternalWebsite\n3. Add to Config file dependencies section\n4. If version set errors occur: first try `brazil ws sync -md`, then `brazil workspace merge` if still needed\n5. Build: `brazil-build release`\n\n## CLI Validation Rules\n\nBefore executing any `brazil*` command, check:\n\n1. **Command exists**: Known `brazil` top-level subcommands include: `workspace`/`ws`, `versionset`/`vs`, `package`/`pkg`, `packagebuilder`/`pb`, `prefs`, `s3binary`/`s3`, `sandbox-build`, `setup`, `version`. Do NOT invent subcommands like `brazil merge`. If unsure whether a subcommand exists, run `brazil --help` to verify.\n2. **Correct nesting**: It's `brazil workspace merge`, NOT `brazil merge`. It's `brazil versionset print`, NOT `brazil print`.\n3. **Flag validity**: When unsure if a flag exists, run `<command> --help` first. Do NOT invent flags.\n4. **Working directory**: `brazil-build`, `brazil-path`, and `brazil-context package` subcommands MUST run from inside a package directory. `brazil ws` commands work from anywhere in the workspace.\n5. **Build system awareness**: `brazil-build` has NO flags of its own — it passes all arguments to the build system. The first argument is often called the \"target\" (e.g., in `brazil-build release`, `release` is the target). Do NOT use flag syntax like `brazil-build --target release`.\n6. **Interactive commands**: `brazil setup` (all variants) and `brazil ws delete` require interactive input and will hang or prompt in automated environments. Do NOT run these commands directly — instruct the user to run them manually in their terminal. See the \"Interactive Commands\" section for the full list.\n\n## Interactive Commands (Do NOT Run Directly)\n\nThese commands require interactive input and MUST NOT be run by the agent:\n\n- `brazil setup` (all variants: `--java`, `--node`, `--python`, `--all`, `--core`)\n- `brazil setup platform-support` — interactive by default. Instruct the user to run it in their terminal with `--force --mode overlay` (macOS) or `--force --mode legacy` (Windows) to skip prompts.\n- `brazil ws delete` — prompts for confirmation (warn user about data loss)\n\nThe agent MAY run `brazil ws remove --force -p <pkg>` only after the user has explicitly confirmed they want the package removed.\n\nWhen the user needs to run any interactive command, instruct them to run it manually in their terminal.\n\n## Output Handling Rules\n\n| Tool | Handling |\n|---|---|\n| `brazil-build`, `brazil-recursive-cmd` | Redirect to log file, tail last 50 lines |\n| `brazil-runtime-exec`, `brazil-farm` | Redirect to log file, tail last 50 lines |\n| `brazil-package-cache` | Read stdout directly (small JSON); check stderr on failure |\n| `brazil ws`, `brazil vs`, `brazil pkg`, `brazil pb`, `brazil-context`, `brazil-path` | Read output directly |\n\n## Decision Tree: Problem → Command\n\n| Problem | Command |\n|---|---|\n| Build single package | `brazil-build release` (from package dir) |\n| Build all packages | `brazil-recursive-cmd --allPackages brazil-build release` |\n| Find workspace root | `brazil-context workspace root` |\n| Find package name | `brazil-context package name` |\n| Check dependency model (brazil vs peru) | `brazil-context workspace model` |\n| Find current platform | `brazil-context workspace platform` |\n| View build dependency graph | `brazil-path build.show-graph` (from package dir) |\n| View runtime dependency graph | `brazil-path run.show-graph` (from package dir) |\n| Java classpath | `brazil-path build.classpath` (from package dir) |\n| Python path | `brazil-path build.pythonpath.python<version>` (from package dir, e.g. `python3.11`) |\n| Add missing dependency | Edit Config, then `brazil ws sync -md`, then `brazil ws merge` if still needed |\n| Resolve version conflicts | `gk-fix-mv-conflicts` (requires Gordian Knot — if command not found, tell user to install via `toolbox install gordian-knot`) |\n| Is package in version set? | `brazil vs print --versionSet <VS>` and grep, or `brazil pkg listversionsets --package <Pkg> --majorVersion <MV>` |\n| Check build request status | `brazil pb view --buildRequest <id>` |\n| Submit dry-run build | `brazil ws dryrun` |\n| Sync workspace to latest | `brazil ws sync` |\n| Run cmd in runtime env (classic) | `brazil-runtime-exec <cmd>` |\n| Run cmd in runtime env (Peru) | `brazil-farm -p <pkg> -c <closure>` |\n\n## brazil-context\n\nPrefer `brazil-context` over `findup` and `get-current-platform` — it runs faster by avoiding unnecessary loading of version set files. However, some queries need to load the version set, and that is where `brazil-path` is useful.\n\nNote: `brazil-context package` subcommands must be run from inside a package directory.\n\nSee **[CLI Commands](references/cli-commands.md)** for the full `brazil-context` command listing.\n\n## brazil-path Quick Reference\n\nMUST run from inside a package directory. Syntax: `brazil-path <graph>.<recipe>`\n\nSee **[CLI Commands](references/cli-commands.md)** for common recipes. For anything beyond those, run `brazil-path --help` or `brazil-path -list` from inside a package directory, or see https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/docs/brazil/user-guide/tools-brazil-path/\n\n## Quick Reference\n\n| Task | Command |\n|------|---------|\n| Create workspace | `brazil workspace create --name NAME --versionSet VS/name` |\n| Use package | `brazil workspace use --package PackageName` |\n| Show workspace | `brazil workspace show` |\n| Build package | `brazil-build release` |\n| Run tests | `brazil-build test` |\n| Clean build | `brazil-build clean` |\n| Fix conflicts | `gk-fix-mv-conflicts` |\n| Sync metadata | `brazil ws sync -md` |\n| Merge dependencies | `brazil workspace merge` |\n| Print version set | `brazil versionset print --versionSet NAME` |\n\n## Common Mistakes\n\n### Building from Workspace Root\n**Problem:** Running `brazil-build` from workspace root instead of package directory.\n**Fix:** For a single package, always `cd src/PackageName` before building. To build all packages, run `brazil-recursive-cmd --allPackages brazil-build` from anywhere in the brazil workspace.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
